---
title: Visualization Design Report
---



This report documents the visual encoding and design decisions made during the development of the visualization product. It translates the visualization concept outlined in the [Project Charta](project_charta.qmd) into concrete design specifications and documents the current prototype visualizations.

## Design Overview

The visualization product focuses on police-recorded road traffic accidents in the Canton of Zurich from 2011 to 2025. The goal is to help users understand temporal, categorical, severity-related and spatial accident patterns.

The current prototype consists of static visualizations generated in Python and embedded into the Quarto documentation. The product is currently designed as a guided visual data story rather than a fully interactive dashboard. The charts are arranged from overview to detail and serve as a first prototype for the final visualization product.

The visualizations aim to help users:

- identify temporal trends in accident frequency
- compare the most common accident types
- understand when accidents occur during the week
- compare accident severity across road types
- make fatal accidents visible as a rare but important outcome
- identify spatial accident hotspots across the Canton of Zurich

## Data-to-Visual Mapping

The visualizations are based on the processed dataset described in the [Data Report](data_report.qmd). The processed dataset contains selected variables such as accident year, accident type, severity category, road type, weekday, hour and accident coordinates.

: Data-to-visual mapping overview. {#tbl-encoding-overview}

| View / Chart | Data variables | Mark type | Visual channels | Scale |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Accident trend over time | `AccidentYear`, accident count | line, points | x-position, y-position, peak annotation | temporal / linear |
| Top accident types | `AccidentType_en`, accident count | horizontal bars | y-position, bar length, value labels | categorical / linear |
| Weekday-hour heatmap | `AccidentWeekDay_en`, `AccidentHour`, accident count | cells | x-position, y-position, colour intensity | categorical / ordinal / linear |
| Severity by road type | `RoadType_en`, `AccidentSeverityCategory_en`, accident count | stacked bars | x-position, bar height, colour category | categorical / linear |
| Fatal accidents by road type | `RoadType_en`, fatal accident count | bars | x-position, bar height | categorical / linear |
| Spatial accident hotspots | `AccidentLocation_CHLV95_E`, `AccidentLocation_CHLV95_N`, accident count | hexbins | x-position, y-position, colour intensity | spatial / logarithmic |

## Layout and Composition

The current prototype is organized as a guided visual story. Each visualization is presented with a short key finding and a short explanation of the visual rhetoric behind the chart.

The views are arranged from general to more specific:

1. overall accident trend over time
2. most frequent accident types
3. weekly and hourly accident patterns
4. severity distribution by road type
5. fatal accidents as a focused deep dive
6. spatial accident hotspots

This order helps users first understand the overall scale and development of accident counts before moving toward more specific categorical, temporal, severity-related and spatial patterns.

## Interaction Design

The current prototype consists of static charts. It does not yet include implemented filters, tooltips or linked views.

Potential future extensions could include:

- filtering by year
- filtering by severity category
- filtering by accident type
- filtering by involvement of pedestrians, bicycles or motorcycles
- tooltips for details-on-demand
- an interactive map after coordinate conversion

These features are considered optional extensions. The current project focus is a reproducible guided data story with clear static visualizations.

## Visual Style and Aesthetics

The current prototype uses a dark visual style with high-contrast charts. The dark background supports visual focus and makes highlighted peaks, dense areas and severity categories stand out.

The visual style follows these principles:

- clear chart titles and subtitles
- readable axis labels and value labels
- consistent dark background across charts
- warm accent colours for highlights and important categories
- colour gradients for frequency or density
- categorical colours for severity levels

Colour is not used only decoratively. In the current prototype, it has different roles depending on the visualization:

- in the accident type chart, colour supports visual hierarchy and frequency ranking
- in the heatmap, colour intensity shows accident frequency by weekday and hour
- in the hexbin plot, colour intensity shows spatial accident concentration
- in the severity charts, colour separates severity categories

## Prototype Description

The current prototype consists of six static visualizations generated in the notebook:

```text
data_acquisition/viz_design/01_visualizations.ipynb
```

The generated images are stored in:

```text
docs/pics/visualizations/
```

### Accident Counts per Year

This line chart shows the development of recorded traffic accidents over time. A highlighted peak supports the narrative of rising accident counts before stabilization.


