## Design Rationale and Iterations

The initial design focused on simple and familiar chart types because the target users include both data-aware students and non-expert users. Line charts and bar charts are easy to read and allow users to quickly identify trends and distributions.

During the design process, the prototype evolved from basic charts to a more consistent visual style. The charts now use a common dark background, clearer titles, subtitles and more deliberate colour choices.

The weekday-hour heatmap was chosen because it allows two temporal dimensions to be shown at the same time. This makes it easier to identify recurring time periods with higher accident frequency.

The stacked severity chart shows both accident volume and severity composition. Because fatal accidents are rare, an additional focused chart was added to make this outcome readable without being hidden inside the larger stacked bars.

A map-based visualization was considered as part of the overall visualization concept. However, the coordinates in the dataset use the Swiss CHLV95 coordinate system. As an intermediate solution, a hexbin plot was created using the available coordinates. This allows spatial hotspots to be shown without requiring a full web-map implementation.

## Conclusions and Next Steps

The current prototype confirms that the processed dataset supports several meaningful visualization perspectives. The charts provide a guided overview of temporal, categorical, severity-related and spatial accident patterns.

The next steps are:

- refine chart annotations and explanatory text
- ensure consistent wording between slides, documentation and figures
- decide whether the final product should remain a guided data story or include simple interactivity
- evaluate whether a full interactive map is feasible within the project timeline
- prepare the final Quarto website for online publication
